# Copy to docker/.env — which is gitignored, and must stay that way.
#
#   cp docker/.env.example docker/.env
#
# Or put it anywhere outside the checkout and point at it, so the repository
# holds no configuration of yours at all:
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/riff && cp docker/.env.example ~/.config/riff/env
#   export RIFF_ENV=~/.config/riff/env      # in your shell profile
#
# Both are read if both exist, with RIFF_ENV winning. This file holds ordinary
# compose settings only — no credentials.
#
# THE RUNTIME CREDENTIAL IS NOT HERE. The agents' Claude token is set once in the
# console (Riff Settings) and lives encrypted in the keyproxy's vault — never in
# this file, an env var, an image layer, or a company's world. Get one with
# `claude setup-token` and paste it there. See SECURITY.md.

# Where companies live on the HOST. A bind mount, so every company is ordinary
# files on your disk whether the container is running or not — and it is what
# docker/backup.sh copies. Defaults to ~/.riff.
# RIFF_DATA=/Users/you/.riff

# Who the first company is, if the installation is empty when it boots. Once a
# company exists these do nothing; renaming one is a console action, because
# changing a name mid-life orphans everything already attributed to it.
# RIFF_COMPANY=Untitled Company
# RIFF_BUSINESS=
# RIFF_CEO=CEO
# RIFF_CHAIR=Chair

# The console, published on loopback only.
# PORT=4173

# Ownership of the files written into the mount. Docker Desktop maps this for
# you; on rootful Linux it does not, and a mismatch is what makes /data
# unwritable. The entrypoint says so plainly rather than failing later.
# UID=1000
# GID=1000

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT set here, on purpose:
#
#   Which hosts are refused           docker/proxy/denylist.conf
#     One host per line, subdomains need their own entry. It is compiled into
#     anchored regexes at BUILD time, so the running proxy image is read-only
#     and the list cannot be edited from inside the box. Rebuild to change it.
#
#   The daily spend cap               src/policy/rules.ts (dailyCapCents)
#     Part of the company's constitution rather than its environment, because
#     it is one of the six rules the staff are told about and can quote back.
